I really like the bit about dropping rings down the drain to determine
the type.  I think it would be nice to have similar things for other
types of objects.  For instance, what about burning scrolls, and using
the color of the flame or smoke, or maybe other effects (i.e. telepor-
tation might cause a `flame nearby'.  Burning scrolls of enchant
armor/weapon might make your armor/weapon hot.  If you don't want to 
give it away with one burnt scroll (after all, scrolls are more 
plentiful than rings, so destroying one isn't so expensive), you could
assign each type of scroll a set of flame colors, one of which is
chosen randomly each time you burn one.  Thus a `the scroll burns with
a red flame' may help narrow it down, but you'd have to burn a few
more to pin it down. (E.g. a scroll of magic mapping could be the only
one that can burn red, blue, or orange.)  Incidentally, a couple years
ago when I added a bunch of stuff to pc-hack (unofficially, of course, 
I didn't know about the officials..) I changed the effect of a scroll
of fire a bit.  Just like reading scrolls of enchant/damage weapon/armor
when confused turned the effect into rustproof/de-rustproof, reading
a scroll of fire when confused would cause it to flameproof the 
scrolls you already had.  (Code people: I just used the rustproof flag..)
I think this would be neat in nh.  It would make scrolls resist fire
breathing dragons better, but would obviously make the identification
scheme above not work.  Potions already have some of this - you can smash
it and smell the fumes, or dip things in poison. It would be neat to 
add more chemistry, though.  Mixing chemicals could have lots of
possibilities - similar chemicals would have boring results, somewhat
different chemicals would react a little and give you a potion of
(salt) water, or maybe toxic waste.  Maybe very different chemicals 
would[A[A react violently - with the damage that entails.  If a person
mixed them in his hands, he'd suffer the damage.  But if he threw them
at a wall or monster, and they broke and mixed there, he could blast
new tunnels or hurt the monster.  Incidentally, I suppose sinks would be
a good place to mix chemicals, if you could find the plug...  But maybe
pouring toxic stuff down the drain would get a big green monster mad at
you.  I can't think of anything to do with wands.  Since amulets are 
similar to rings, how about being able to drop them down sinks, too?  
Gems: how about holding them up to the light (lamp)?  (Speaking of 
lights - how about purple ones?  They wouldn't attack you, but if you 
attacked them you'd get a shock - like those bug-zappers.  Of course, 
having a tame one follow you around would be a great way to get rid of 
things like bees and such.  But having one on a leash when you fall 
into the water would be bad.  (If people ever do anything that's been 
discussed about non-lethal pools.)  White lights could be like yellow 
ones, but with moths hovering around.  While you're at it, how about 
having your lamp attract moths, unless it's the more expensive yellow 
type?)
	Also, how about having more magic based on the appearance of
objects.  I think I already mentioned how in my hack modifications
I made vampires fear wooden things, like rings and wands.  Wielding
a wooden ring at one would scare it away, wooden wands would too with
the added possibility of spearing one through the heart.  In nh, you
could make were-beasts fear silver wands and rings.  Of course, if you
polymorphed into a were-beast while having a silver object could be
dangerous, to say nothing of wielding or wearing something silver.
	Question: if you polymorph into a vaporous beast (or anything
small), then drop a ring of hunger down a drain (which makes the drain 
suck things), shouldn't you get sucked into the drain?  Maybe you could 
follow the drain and pop out of another drain.  Of course, polymorphing
to something large while still in the drain would be non-habit-forming..
	Question 2: Why don't vampires turn into bats, like were-beasts?
(Or have I just not come across this yet - it seems to obvious to be
left out.)
	I like the recent suggestions for expanding the water things -
various (not necessarily deadly) pools, new monsters and tools to go
along.  Maybe a water level would have submerged passages, and rooms
you'd have to swim to get to.  If somebody adds `puddles' or shallow
pools, then maybe you should leave wet footprints after walking through
it?  And/or make squishing sounds to wake up monsters?  The footprints
would make it easier for animals to track you.  Except your dog, of
course, since it'd wipe out the smell.  Speaking of which, why don't
Keystone Kops have police dogs?
	I also like the `mall' idea.  I don't think it's anachronistic,
only the all-encompassing permanent roof and flourescent lighting are
modern..  Incidentally, the pushy, obnoxious customers would be out
of place.  In California, `the home of the mini-mall', people are
*nice* to one another, even strangers.  (This drives visiting New
Yorkers batty!)  Having all those customers milling about would probably
make the game very slow, however.  I cite the people who argue against
tame monsters because of the time they take up on slow machines.
Somebody said pets wouldn't be allowed on the mall level.  But seeing-eye
dogs are always allowed, so if you had a blindfold on and your dog on a 
leash, you should be able to take it past the guard no problem.  After
you're out of his sight you could take off the blindfold and leash.
I don't think the arcade bit would fit - (I *hope* you didn't mean
the player would actually play an arcade-type game! (Gee, let's triple
the size of nh!)) - but maybe there could be oracle-like denizens
sitting on the benches.  Speaking ValSpeak, of course.  ("Like, if
you don't dig statues, you totally won't appreciate them, like y'know?"
(With apologies to dave.))  While we're at it, the information booth
might be a good place to get information - including maps and such - 
particularly for that level.  Maybe by blessing (or some other way
of adding magic to) a mall map, you could make a magic mapping scroll.
As for credit cards - paying your bill (at the mall's bank) would not 
only appease any shopkeepers you used your card on, it would increase
your credit limit.  This way you could keep your gold and gems in a
chest - or vault - and pay the bank.  I think fountains should have
some coins in them.  Of course, guards might not like you getting them,
and you have the same problems with getting wet.  Taking off all of
your clothes is no answer, however!  That would get the guards *really*
upset.
	Let's keep bouncing these ideas for new things about.  Maybe we
can work out some nice thorough ideas to present to the heroic dev team,
or which can be implemented by some foolhardy^H^H^H^H^H^H^H kindhearted
soul.  I think the `confirm' debate is an encouraging paradigm.
